  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Above normal temperatures and dry conditions \nwill prevail through Friday. \n\nAloft, prominent ridging will build in from along the Gulf \nCoast and settle directly across the forecast area by Thursday. \nThe ridge will then break down into Friday as a sharp trough \nmoves into the Upper Midwest and the Great Lakes region. At the \nsurface, dry high pressure will sit across the Southeast region \nthrough Thursday before retreating offshore Friday as a fast \nmoving cold front approaches from the northwest. The theme for \nthe week will be rain- free conditions and well above normal \ntemperatures. The setup will support widespread mid to upper 80s\neach day, which would be on the order of 7-9 degrees above \nnormal for mid October. In fact, a few isolated locations \nreaching 90 is in play almost each day and we could be near \ndaily records at times (see Climate section below). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A strong cold front will yield breezy and well-\nbelow normal temperatures this weekend.\n\nA strong cold front will pass through the region late Friday \nnight into Saturday morning, yielding afternoon temperatures \ninto the upper 60s and low 70s on Saturday and Sunday. This \ncombined with dewpoints in the upper 40s to low 50s will lead to\nfairly nice fall- like weekend. Also, inland surface ridging \nwill yield breezy north- northeasterly flow at 15-20 mph with \ngusts up to 25-30 mph on Saturday and Sunday. This surface ridge\nwill slide offshore of the New England coastline on Monday, \nresulting in the breezy winds to slowly subside and shift more \neasterly. Temperatures on Monday will warm back up into the mid \nto upper 70s.",
